WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.322
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.322 --> 00:00:12.414
What we found was that organizations that
had strong norms for

00:00:12.414 --> 00:00:17.080
adaptability that were widely shared and
strongly held.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.410
Those organizations perform much better.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.620
They perform better in terms of net income
growth.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.350
They perform better in terms of revenue
growth.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:30.190
They perform better in terms of the
market-to-book value of the company.

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:33.060
They perform better in terms of employee
ratings.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.910
And they perform better in terms of a,
analyst recommendations to buy the stock.

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:43.167
So this was, this is really the first
study that demonstrates, conclusively,

00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:48.505
that there are very clear relationships
between culture in an organization, and

00:00:48.505 --> 00:00:49.843
firm performance.

00:00:49.843 --> 00:00:53.268
[MUSIC]

00:00:53.268 --> 00:00:56.740
It includes things like willingness to
experiment.

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:58.710
Being quick to opportunities.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.100
Being flexible.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.780
Being willing to tolerate some failure.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:08.990
So, all these are specific behaviors, that
I think managers can be thinking about,

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:12.540
when they're encouraging people to, to, to
be adaptable.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:16.740
So, it's not just a, a global label that,
that means nothing.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:20.082
It has some very concrete meaning, that
can be managed.

00:01:20.082 --> 00:01:23.750
[MUSIC]

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:28.837
When managers think about culture in
organizations, they should be sensitive to

00:01:28.837 --> 00:01:32.890
the fact that what they're really trying
to do are create norms,

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:37.829
create expectations among employees around
this notion of adaptability, and

00:01:37.829 --> 00:01:42.925
to make sure that those norms are widely
shared within the organization.

00:01:42.925 --> 00:01:46.190
We've actually looked at how managers
create cultures and

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:50.360
there are really 4 big mechanisms that
managers use.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:53.570
First of all, the signals that they send,
that is,

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:56.160
the questions they ask, where they spend
their time.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:00.310
So, what are the messages that they are
sending, through both their words and

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:01.070
their, and their actions.

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:04.850
The second lever that we talk about is the
involvement of people,

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:06.180
getting people engaged so

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:10.390
that people understand that what they're,
what they're doing is important.

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:13.440
The third big lever that managers used to

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:18.260
shape culture has to do with the vivid
illustrations that they send to people,

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:24.280
sort of what gets held up as exemplary,
what, what is recognized and approved of.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:27.410
And the fourth lever is, has to do with
rewards.

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:28.562
But typically not money.

00:02:28.562 --> 00:02:31.410
Money is not a particularly good way to
shape culture.

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:35.410
When we talk about rewards, we're really
talking about what gets recognized,

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:36.760
what gets approved.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:38.840
I mean, one of the companies we looked at,

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:43.680
they, they explicitly talk about honoring
people who share the values.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.930
So, think about those four levers as a
manager.

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:51.110
Then, what I can do is I can use these
four levers to think about what

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:52.810
are the signals I'm sending about.

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:55.930
Is it okay to explore new avenues?

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:57.800
Is it okay to take the initiative?

00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:59.410
Is it okay to be fast?

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:00.330
Is it okay?

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:06.060
And if you use those four levers and you
constantly repeat the messages,

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.940
then people will begin to understand that,
look, this is important.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:12.660
You know, Jack Welch said something
interesting about culture.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:17.330
He said, most managers fail at managing
culture because they get bored.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:21.150
I mean we, we give the same talk five
times and we want to give another talk.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:25.480
And what Will says, if you want to be
affective at managing culture,

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:27.080
you've got to be two things as a manager.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:30.130
You have to be relentless, you have to be
boring.

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:34.400
What he meant by that is, you have to
repeat again, again, and

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.020
again these messages.

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:41.530
So the extent in which managers in
organizations constantly

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:45.990
reinforce to their employees that we want
you to, to take small risks.

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:48.130
We want you to try new things.

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:51.260
We want you to sort of take the
initiative.

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:54.709
That's what creates this, this culture of
adaptability.

00:03:54.709 --> 00:03:57.974
[MUSIC]

00:03:57.974 --> 00:04:02.830
Culture can be a real competitive
advantage or a competitive disadvantage.

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:05.600
And a question I think for managers, is to

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.510
really think hard about what the culture
is that you need to execute your strategy?

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:14.840
In that process, the culture that works
for

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:19.740
today's strategy may not work as the world
changes.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:22.790
And therefore, the key to long term
success is to

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:25.750
have a culture that's aligned with your
strategy but

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:29.860
also has embedded in it, norms that
promote adaptability.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:33.370
If you, if you think about organizations
that survive for

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:37.075
long periods, they're around today only
because they had

00:04:37.075 --> 00:04:42.260
leaders who were able to help them succeed
in the short term in one environment.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:49.600
And then as the world changed to help them
shift to a new a new alignment of culture.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:55.098
And at, the norms for adaptability are
what we believe help them do that.

00:04:55.098 --> 00:05:04.549
[MUSIC]

